sections.s2_technical:
String-based JPQL is fine for fixed queries, but dynamic filtering is painful: you end up concatenating `WHERE` fragments and juggling `AND`/`OR`, which is error-prone and injection-risky if done naively. The Criteria API builds the query from objects. `CriteriaBuilder` is the factory; `CriteriaQuery<T>` is the query; `Root<T>` represents the entity; and each condition is a `Predicate`. You add only the predicates that apply.

```java
CriteriaBuilder cb = em.getCriteriaBuilder();
CriteriaQuery<Account> cq = cb.createQuery(Account.class);
Root<Account> root = cq.from(Account.class);
List<Predicate> predicates = new ArrayList<>();
if (minBalance != null) predicates.add(cb.ge(root.get("balance"), minBalance));
if (type != null)       predicates.add(cb.equal(root.get("type"), type));
cq.where(cb.and(predicates.toArray(new Predicate[0])));
List<Account> result = em.createQuery(cq).getResultList();
```

Type safety improves further with the **JPA metamodel** (`Account_.balance`), generated at compile time, so a renamed field breaks the build instead of failing at runtime like the string `"balance"` would.

Spring Data `Specification<T>` wraps this pattern cleanly. A `Specification` is a functional interface with one method `toPredicate(root, query, criteriaBuilder)`. You write small specifications per filter and compose them:

```java
public static Specification<Account> hasType(String type) {
    return (root, q, cb) -> type == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("type"), type);
}
public static Specification<Account> balanceAtLeast(BigDecimal min) {
    return (root, q, cb) -> min == null ? null : cb.ge(root.get("balance"), min);
}
// compose — null specs are ignored, so optional filters just drop out
Specification<Account> spec = Specification.where(hasType(type)).and(balanceAtLeast(min));
List<Account> result = accountRepo.findAll(spec);
```

Returning `null` from a specification when its input is absent means that filter is simply skipped — this is the elegant answer to optional filters. The repository extends `JpaSpecificationExecutor<Account>` to get `findAll(Specification)`, which also supports paging and sorting.

Senior nuances: specifications are reusable and testable in isolation, and compose into complex boolean logic with `and`/`or`/`not`. Use the generated metamodel for full compile-time safety. Criteria can do joins (`root.join(...)`), aggregations, and projections into DTOs (`cq.select(cb.construct(...))`). The trade-off is verbosity and lower readability than JPQL for *static* queries, so I reserve Criteria/Specification for genuinely dynamic queries and keep fixed queries as `@Query`/named queries. Watch for `fetch` joins in specifications causing pagination issues, and avoid building predicates from unvalidated raw input field names.

